  entries: [
    {"id":"bourbon","name":"Bourbon","respell":"BUR-bun","family":"american","terms":["bourbon"],"neighbour":"tennessee-whiskey","law":"27 CFR 5.143, the United States standards of identity: bourbon whisky is made in the United States from a fermented mash of at least 51 per cent corn, distilled at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof), stored in charred new oak containers at no more than 62.5% ABV (125 proof) and bottled at 40% ABV (80 proof) or more, with no added colouring, flavouring or blending materials. Bourbon itself has no minimum age; straight bourbon needs at least two years.","made":"A mash of at least 51 per cent corn, with rye or wheat as the flavouring grain and malted barley for its enzymes, is cooked, cooled and fermented with the house's chosen yeast. It is usually distilled first in a column still and then in a doubler or thumper, and goes into new charred oak no stronger than 62.5%. Recipe, yeast, char, warehouse and years are where one house parts from another.","taste":"Vanilla, caramel and toffee from the new charred oak over sweet corn, baked apple or dark cherry and baking spice; round and sweet on the palate, drying to oak and pepper. Young bourbon runs bright and grainy, older bourbon leans to leather, tobacco and dark fruit, and a rye recipe usually reads spicier than a wheated one.","lines":{"s10":"Bourbon is American whiskey made mostly from corn and aged in new charred oak: vanilla, caramel and baking spice.","s20":"Bourbon can be made anywhere in the United States, not only Kentucky, from at least 51 per cent corn, aged in new charred oak with nothing added but water. Expect vanilla, caramel and sweet corn, then baking spice and oak.","s45":"This is bourbon, the American whiskey of corn and new oak. The federal rule asks for at least 51 per cent corn, distillation no higher than 160 proof and new charred oak barrels filled at no more than 125 proof, with nothing added but water, anywhere in the United States. Rye or wheat joins the corn, the spirit usually runs through a column still and a doubler, and the new oak gives vanilla, caramel and toffee over sweet corn and baking spice. We pour it neat, on one large cube, or in an Old Fashioned. Congress named it a distinctive product of the United States in 1964.","sale":"Bourbon is corn and new charred oak, so vanilla and caramel come first and the spice follows. Try it neat with a drop of water, or in an Old Fashioned."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, so the vanilla and sweet corn come forward.","water":"A few drops of still water open the caramel and calm a higher proof.","rocks":"One large cube chills it slowly; small ice waters it down too fast.","highball":"Long with ginger ale or soda over ice: the corn sweetness holds up to the bubbles.","glass":"A Glencairn or tulip neat; a rocks glass for ice and the Old Fashioned; a highball glass long.","classic":"An Old Fashioned first; then a Mint Julep, Whiskey Sour, Boulevardier or Gold Rush."},"guest":[{"q":"Does bourbon have to come from Kentucky?","a":"No. The federal rule asks only that it be made in the United States. Kentucky is its home ground, but bourbon is made in many states."},{"q":"How old does bourbon have to be?","a":"Bourbon itself has no minimum age by law. Straight bourbon needs at least two years in new charred oak, and a whiskey under four years must state its age on the label."},{"q":"Is there sugar or vanilla added?","a":"No. Bourbon allows nothing but water. The vanilla, caramel and sweetness come from the corn and from the new charred oak, which gives up its sugars and vanillin to the spirit."}],"compare":"Tennessee whiskey keeps rules close to bourbon's, at least 51 per cent corn and new charred oak, but under a state law of 2013, Tennessee Code 57-2-106: made in Tennessee and filtered through maple charcoal before ageing, with one exception. The charcoal is often said to round off the new spirit; side by side the two can be close.","benchmarks":["Buffalo Trace","Wild Turkey","Four Roses","Old Forester","Jim Beam","Heaven Hill"],"shelf":["ofc-2005"],"chapters":["bourbon","american-labels"],"canon":["old-fashioned","mint-julep","whiskey-sour","boulevardier","gold-rush"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What does the federal rule require of bourbon, and what does it not require?","answer":"Made in the United States from at least 51 per cent corn, distilled at no more than 80% ABV, put into new charred oak at no more than 62.5%, bottled at 40% or more, nothing added but water. It requires neither Kentucky nor a minimum age; straight bourbon needs two years."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"What share of corn must a bourbon mash hold, at the least?","options":["51 per cent","60 per cent","75 per cent","80 per cent"],"a":0,"explain":"At least 51 per cent corn, under 27 CFR 5.143. Eighty per cent is the corn whiskey line, and corn whiskey goes into used or uncharred oak, never new charred barrels."},{"id":"q2","q":"At what strength, at most, may bourbon go into its new charred oak barrel?","options":["160 proof","125 proof","110 proof","100 proof"],"a":1,"explain":"No more than 125 proof, 62.5% ABV. 160 proof, 80% ABV, is the ceiling at the still, and 100 proof is the bottled-in-bond strength."},{"id":"q3","q":"In which year did Congress name bourbon a distinctive product of the United States?","options":["1897","1935","1964","1971"],"a":2,"explain":"1964, by a concurrent resolution agreed by both houses in May. 1897 is the year of the Bottled-in-Bond Act."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.143"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.74, Statements of age","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.74"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: Age statement","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/age-statement/"},{"title":"United States Statutes at Large, 78 Stat. 1208: the concurrent resolution on bourbon whiskey, 1964","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-78/pdf/STATUTE-78-Pg1208.pdf"},{"title":"History, Art and Archives, United States House of Representatives: Designating Bourbon Whiskey","url":"https://history.house.gov/HouseRecord/Detail/15032436199"},{"title":"Distillery Trail: Bourbon declared a distinctive product of the United States","url":"https://www.distillerytrail.com/blog/when-did-bourbon-whiskey-become-a-distinctive-product-of-the-united-states/"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: Doublers and thumpers are the whiskey maker's little helpers","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/Whiskey-Distillation-Doublers-and-Thumpers-Explained"},{"title":"Alcademics: How column distillation works, bourbon edition","url":"https://alcademics.com/how-column-distillation-works-bourbon-edition/"},{"title":"Justia: Tennessee Code 57-2-106, Tennessee whiskey","url":"https://law.justia.com/codes/tennessee/2019/title-57/chapter-2/section-57-2-106/"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Bourbon","url":""},{"title":"Cask Atlas: American Labels","url":""}]},
    {"id":"wheated-bourbon","name":"Wheated bourbon","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["wheated bourbon","wheated","wheater"],"neighbour":"high-rye-bourbon","law":"No legal category of its own: a wheated bourbon is bourbon whose second grain is wheat rather than rye. The rule is bourbon's, 27 CFR 5.143: made in the United States from a mash of at least 51 per cent corn, distilled at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof), put into charred new oak at no more than 62.5% ABV (125 proof), bottled at 40% ABV or more, with no added colouring, flavouring or blending materials. Straight bourbon adds at least two years in that oak.","made":"Wheat takes the place of rye as the flavouring grain beside corn and malted barley. Published recipes run around 68 to 70 per cent corn and 16 to 20 per cent wheat, the rest malted barley: Maker's Mark's is given as 70, 16 and 14, with soft red winter wheat, and Larceny's as 68, 20 and 12. Other houses keep their figures private. Yeast, fermentation, the still, entry proof, the warehouse and age shape the spirit as much as the grain does.","taste":"Wheat is often found softer and rounder: honey, caramel, vanilla and fresh bread on the nose, soft red fruit and butterscotch on the palate, and less of the pepper and dill a rye recipe can bring. The grain is one influence among yeast, barrel, proof and age, so an older or barrel proof wheater can still run rich and hot.","lines":{"s10":"A wheated bourbon is bourbon made with wheat in place of rye beside the corn: often softer and rounder, honey and caramel rather than spice.","s20":"Wheated bourbon is still bourbon by law, at least 51 per cent corn and new charred oak, but its second grain is wheat rather than rye. It is often found softer and rounder: honey, caramel, vanilla and fresh bread, with less pepper.","s45":"This is a wheated bourbon. It is bourbon under the federal rule, made in the United States from at least 51 per cent corn and aged in new charred oak, but the second grain is wheat where most recipes use rye. Wheat is often found softer and rounder: expect honey, caramel, vanilla and fresh bread, and less of the pepper a rye recipe brings, though barrel and age matter as much. We pour it neat, with a few drops of water, or over one large cube, and it makes a gentle Old Fashioned. Stitzel-Weller, opened near Louisville in 1935, made it a house style.","sale":"Wheated bourbon trades the rye for wheat, so it is often softer and rounder: honey, caramel and fresh bread rather than spice. Try it neat with a drop of water, or in an Old Fashioned for a guest who finds bourbon sharp."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn or a small tulip, so the honey and bread come forward.","water":"A few drops of still water open the caramel and soften a higher proof.","rocks":"One large cube; small ice waters a softer whiskey down too fast.","glass":"A Glencairn or small tulip neat; a rocks glass for the cube or the Old Fashioned.","classic":"An Old Fashioned, or a Gold Rush where honey meets honey; a Whiskey Sour or Mint Julep too."},"guest":[{"q":"Is wheated bourbon sweeter?","a":"It is often found softer and rounder, with honey and bread rather than pepper, but nothing is added: bourbon allows only water. Barrel, proof and age can still make a wheater rich or hot."},{"q":"Is it the same as wheat whiskey?","a":"No. Wheated bourbon is at least 51 per cent corn with wheat as the second grain; wheat whiskey is at least 51 per cent wheat, a separate type under the same federal rule."},{"q":"Why use wheat instead of rye?","a":"It is the distiller's choice of character: rye brings spice and a drier edge, wheat a gentler, bready sweetness. Stitzel-Weller built its bourbons on wheat from 1935, and Maker's Mark chose soft red winter wheat."}],"compare":"Against a high-rye bourbon the rule is the same and only the second grain changes: wheat in place of a large share of rye. The high-rye bourbon leans to pepper, dill and a drier finish, the wheater to honey, bread and a rounder palate. Barrel and age can narrow the gap.","benchmarks":["Maker's Mark","W. L. Weller","Old Fitzgerald","Larceny","Stitzel-Weller"],"shelf":["weller-special-reserve","weller-12"],"chapters":["bourbon"],"canon":["old-fashioned","gold-rush","whiskey-sour","mint-julep"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What makes a bourbon a wheated bourbon, and is wheated bourbon a legal category?","answer":"Wheat replaces rye as the second grain beside corn and malted barley. It is no separate legal category: it is bourbon under 27 CFR 5.143, at least 51 per cent corn, charred new oak, nothing added. Often found softer and rounder, honey and bread rather than pepper."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"In a wheated bourbon, which grain does wheat take the place of?","options":["Rye","Corn","Malted barley","Oats"],"a":0,"explain":"Rye. Corn stays the main grain, at least 51 per cent by law, and malted barley stays for its enzymes; wheat takes rye's place as the flavouring grain."},{"id":"q2","q":"Which distillery, opened near Louisville in 1935, made wheated bourbon its house style?","options":["Old Forester","Heaven Hill","Stitzel-Weller","Four Roses"],"a":2,"explain":"Stitzel-Weller, opened on Derby Day 1935 in Shively, on the edge of Louisville. Heaven Hill also opened in 1935, but in Bardstown."},{"id":"q3","q":"Which wheat does Maker's Mark name as the flavouring grain of its bourbon?","options":["Hard red spring wheat","Durum wheat","Soft white wheat","Soft red winter wheat"],"a":3,"explain":"Soft red winter wheat, in place of the usual rye. Its recipe is given as 70 per cent corn, 16 per cent wheat and 14 per cent malted barley."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.143"},{"title":"Maker's Mark: Maker's Mark bourbon","url":"https://www.makersmark.com/bourbons/makers-mark"},{"title":"Uproxx: What is the Maker's Mark bourbon whiskey mash bill?","url":"https://uproxx.com/life/what-is-makers-mark-mash-bill/"},{"title":"VinePair: Larceny bourbon guide","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/larceny-bourbon-guide/"},{"title":"The Bourbon Culture: Maker's Mark vs Larceny","url":"https://thebourbonculture.com/whiskey-reviews/makers-mark-bourbon-vs-larceny-bourbon-review/"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Stitzel-Weller Distillery","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stitzel-Weller"},{"title":"Whisky Magazine: A short history of Stitzel-Weller","url":"https://whiskymag.com/articles/short-history-of-stitzel-weller"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Bourbon","url":""}]},
    {"id":"high-rye-bourbon","name":"High-rye bourbon","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["high-rye bourbon","high-rye"],"neighbour":"wheated-bourbon","law":"No legal category of its own: high rye is a trade term for a bourbon with a larger share of rye than usual, and no rule fixes the line. The rule is bourbon's, 27 CFR 5.143: made in the United States from at least 51 per cent corn, distilled at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof), stored in charred new oak at no more than 62.5% ABV (125 proof), bottled at 40% ABV or more, nothing added. Straight bourbon adds at least two years.","made":"Corn stays the main grain, at least 51 per cent, with rye as the flavouring grain and malted barley for its enzymes. Most bourbon recipes carry about 8 to 15 per cent rye; a high-rye recipe is often put at about 20 to 35 per cent. Four Roses publishes one of 60 per cent corn, 35 rye and 5 malted barley; Rabbit Hole Heigold's is 70, 25 and 5, its rye malted. Yeast, still, char and age shape the spirit as much.","taste":"The rye shows as spice over bourbon's vanilla and caramel: black pepper, cinnamon, clove and a little mint or dill on the nose, a drier, spicier palate, and a warm, peppery finish. Often found brighter and leaner than a wheated bourbon, though barrel and age can round it.","lines":{"s10":"A high-rye bourbon is bourbon with a larger share of rye beside the corn: vanilla and caramel, then pepper and cinnamon.","s20":"High-rye bourbon is still bourbon, at least 51 per cent corn in new charred oak, but its rye share runs well above the usual, often a fifth to a third. Expect caramel and vanilla, then pepper, cinnamon and a drier finish.","s45":"This is a high-rye bourbon. It is bourbon under the federal rule, made in the United States from at least 51 per cent corn and aged in new charred oak, but it carries far more rye than most recipes, often a fifth to a third of the mash where many bourbons hold less than a sixth. The rye shows as black pepper, cinnamon and clove over the corn's caramel and vanilla, with a drier, spicier finish. We pour it neat, on one large cube, or in a Boulevardier, where it stands up to the Campari. Old Grand-Dad, a Kentucky brand since 1882, is a high-rye name to know.","sale":"High-rye bourbon keeps the corn's caramel and adds the rye's pepper and cinnamon, so it reads drier and livelier. Try it in a Boulevardier or a New York Sour, where the spice holds its own."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, so the spice and the caramel both show.","water":"A few drops of still water soften the pepper and lift the fruit.","rocks":"One large cube rounds the rye without drowning it.","highball":"Long with ginger ale over ice, where the ginger meets the rye spice.","glass":"A Glencairn or tulip neat; a rocks glass for ice and the Old Fashioned.","classic":"A Boulevardier or Old Fashioned, where the spice stands up to bitters; a New York Sour or Paper Plane too."},"guest":[{"q":"Is a high-rye bourbon a rye whiskey?","a":"No. It is still bourbon, at least 51 per cent corn; rye whiskey needs at least 51 per cent rye. High rye only means a larger share of rye beside the corn."},{"q":"How much rye makes it high rye?","a":"There is no legal line. Most bourbons carry about 8 to 15 per cent rye, and the trade often calls about 20 to 35 per cent high rye. Four Roses publishes a recipe of 35 per cent."},{"q":"Will it taste spicy?","a":"Usually spicier than most bourbon: pepper, cinnamon, clove and a little mint over the corn's caramel, with a drier finish. Barrel, proof and age still shape it."}],"compare":"Against a wheated bourbon the rule is the same and only the second grain changes: a large share of rye where the wheater uses wheat. The high-rye bourbon leans to pepper, cinnamon, mint and a drier finish, the wheater to honey, fresh bread and a rounder palate. Barrel and age can narrow the gap.","benchmarks":["Four Roses","Old Grand-Dad","Basil Hayden","Rabbit Hole"],"shelf":["rabbit-hole-heigold"],"chapters":["bourbon"],"canon":["old-fashioned","boulevardier","new-york-sour","paper-plane"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What makes a bourbon a high-rye bourbon, and does any rule fix the line?","answer":"A larger share of rye than usual beside the corn, often about 20 to 35 per cent where most bourbons carry about 8 to 15. No rule fixes the line: it is bourbon under 27 CFR 5.143, at least 51 per cent corn, charred new oak, nothing added."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"What does federal law require of a bourbon called high rye?","options":["At least 20 per cent rye","At least 35 per cent rye","Nothing beyond the bourbon rule","At least 51 per cent rye"],"a":2,"explain":"High rye is a trade term with no legal line; the bourbon rule of 27 CFR 5.143 applies. At least 51 per cent rye would make it rye whiskey, not bourbon."},{"id":"q2","q":"What share of rye does Four Roses publish for the spicier of its two bourbon recipes?","options":["35 per cent","20 per cent","25 per cent","51 per cent"],"a":0,"explain":"35 per cent rye, with 60 per cent corn and 5 per cent malted barley; its other recipe is 20 per cent rye. Rabbit Hole Heigold's is 25 per cent."},{"id":"q3","q":"In which year was Old Grand-Dad, a high-rye bourbon brand, first made in Kentucky?","options":["1897","1882","1935","1964"],"a":1,"explain":"1882, when the founder named it for his grandfather, Basil Hayden. 1897 is the year of the Bottled-in-Bond Act, and 1935 the year Stitzel-Weller opened."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"A bourbon historian's blog: What do the terms high rye and low rye mean? (2011)","url":"https://chuckcowdery.blogspot.com/2011/11/what-do-terns-high-rye-and-low-rye-mean.html"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: What does the industry mean when they say high or low rye bourbon?","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/?p=45886"},{"title":"New Riff Distilling: What is high rye bourbon?","url":"https://newriffdistilling.com/what-is-high-rye-bourbon/"},{"title":"Rabbit Hole Distillery: Heigold, high rye bourbon","url":"https://www.rabbitholedistillery.com/blogs/bourbon-101/heigold-high-rye-bourbon"},{"title":"Rabbit Hole Distillery: Heigold Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","url":"https://www.rabbitholedistillery.com/products/heigold-kentucky-straight-bourbon-whiskey"},{"title":"Boozemakers: Bourbon mash bills explained","url":"https://www.boozemakers.com/articles/bourbon-mash-bills-explained"},{"title":"Four Roses: Our recipes","url":"https://fourrosesbourbon.com/our-recipes"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: How Four Roses' recipes create different bourbon profiles","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/whiskey-articles/how-four-roses-unique-recipes-create-different-bourbon-profiles/"},{"title":"VinePair: Four Roses bourbon recipe guide","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/four-roses-bourbon-recipe-guide/"},{"title":"A bourbon historian's blog: The good and not so good of Old Grand-Dad (2015)","url":"https://chuckcowdery.blogspot.com/2015/02/the-good-and-not-so-good-of-old-grand.html"},{"title":"VinePair: Old Grand-Dad bourbon guide","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/ntk-old-grand-dad-bourbon-guide/"},{"title":"Thirty One Whiskey: Basil Hayden's Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","url":"https://www.thirtyonewhiskey.com/whiskey-review-basil-haydens-kentucky-straight-bourbon-whiskey/"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Bourbon","url":""}]},
    {"id":"islay","name":"Islay","respell":"EYE-luh","family":"scotch","terms":["Islay"],"neighbour":"islands","law":"The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009 protect Islay as a locality, with Campbeltown. A Scotch may carry the name only if it was wholly distilled on the island; it may mature elsewhere in Scotland, and the rule asks nothing about peat. Every Scotch rule holds: distilled and matured in Scotland, at least three years in oak casks of no more than 700 litres, bottled at 40% ABV or more, with nothing added but water and plain caramel.","made":"Islay single malt is malted barley, mashed, fermented and usually distilled twice in copper pot stills at one of the island's distilleries. Most of them dry their malt over peat smoke, and the smoke's phenols cling to the grain and carry through the stills; Bunnahabhain and Bruichladdich make unpeated spirit as well. Most of it matures in ex-bourbon casks, with sherry casks for richer styles, and Islay malt also goes into blends.","taste":"In the peated malts: peat smoke, iodine, sea salt, tar and lemon over vanilla and pear from bourbon casks, with a long, drying, smoky finish; sherry casks add dried fruit and dark chocolate. The unpeated malts are a different glass: malt, nuts, honey and a little salt. Peat level varies from house to house.","lines":{"s10":"Islay is Scotch whisky distilled on the island of Islay, off Scotland's west coast: mostly peated, so smoke, iodine and sea salt.","s20":"Islay whisky is distilled on Islay, one of the two places the Scotch Whisky Regulations protect by name as a locality. Most of its distilleries dry their barley over peat, so expect smoke, iodine, sea salt and lemon; a few make unpeated malt.","s45":"This is an Islay single malt, distilled on the island of Islay in the Inner Hebrides, one of two localities the Scotch Whisky Regulations protect by name. It is malted barley, usually distilled twice in copper pot stills and aged at least three years in oak. Most of the island's distilleries dry their malt over peat smoke, so expect smoke, iodine, sea salt and lemon over vanilla from the cask, and a long, drying finish; a few make unpeated malt. We pour it neat with a jug of water on the side, and a little floats on a Penicillin. Bowmore, on Loch Indaal, dates from 1779.","sale":"Islay is the smoke and the sea in a glass: peat, iodine and salt over lemon and vanilla. Taste it neat with a few drops of water, or have it floated on a Penicillin."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature, so the smoke and the fruit under it both show.","water":"A few drops of still water calm the smoke and bring out lemon, pear and vanilla.","rocks":"One large cube at most; cold mutes the fruit and leaves only the smoke.","highball":"With chilled soda in a tall glass over ice, a smoky highball with a lemon peel.","glass":"A Glencairn or nosing glass neat; a highball glass for the soda; a rocks glass for the Penicillin.","classic":"A Penicillin, as the smoky float on top; a few drops in a Smoky Martini."},"guest":[{"q":"Are all Islay whiskies smoky?","a":"No. Most of the island's distilleries peat their barley, but Bunnahabhain and Bruichladdich make unpeated malts too. The smoke comes from drying the barley over peat, not from the island itself."},{"q":"Where does the smoke come from?","a":"Peat burned to dry the malted barley. Its smoke leaves phenols on the grain that carry through distillation; the cask then adds vanilla and fruit around them."},{"q":"How do you say it?","a":"EYE-luh. It is an island of the Inner Hebrides, off Scotland's west coast."}],"compare":"Against the Islands: Islay is a protected locality in the Scotch Whisky Regulations, while the Islands are an informal group whose whiskies are Highland in law. Both can be smoky, but most Islay houses peat their malt, while island malts such as Highland Park and Talisker range from heather and honey to pepper and smoke.","benchmarks":["Lagavulin","Laphroaig","Ardbeg","Bowmore","Caol Ila","Bruichladdich"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["scotch-regions"],"canon":["penicillin","smoky-martini","whiskey-highball"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What does Islay promise on a Scotch label, and does it promise peat?","answer":"That the whisky was wholly distilled on the island of Islay, a protected locality under the Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, with Campbeltown. It does not promise peat: most Islay distilleries peat their malt, but Bunnahabhain and Bruichladdich make unpeated spirit too. It may mature elsewhere in Scotland."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"Which other place shares Islay's standing as a protected locality under the Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009?","options":["Speyside","Campbeltown","Orkney","Skye"],"a":1,"explain":"Campbeltown. Highland, Lowland and Speyside are protected regions; Orkney and Skye belong to the informal Islands group, which is Highland in law."},{"id":"q2","q":"What gives most Islay single malts their smoky character?","options":["Malted barley dried over burning peat","Casks charred over a peat fire","Peaty water added at bottling","Sea air in the warehouses"],"a":0,"explain":"Peat smoke from drying the malt: its phenols cling to the grain and carry through distillation. Peaty water and sea air are popular stories, and Scotch allows only water and plain caramel to be added."},{"id":"q3","q":"Bowmore, on the shore of Loch Indaal, dates its founding to which year?","options":["1494","1823","1779","1815"],"a":2,"explain":"1779, by most accounts. The distillery stands in the village of Bowmore, on the shore of Loch Indaal."}],"sources":[{"title":"legislation.gov.uk: The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, regulation 10","url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/2890/regulation/10"},{"title":"legislation.gov.uk: The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, regulation 3","url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/2890/regulation/3"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scotch_Whisky_Regulations_2009"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Islay whisky","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islay_whisky"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Bowmore distillery","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bowmore_distillery"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: Bowmore Distillery","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/producers/221/bowmore-distillery"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Scotland, map the place, question the stereotype","url":""}]},
    {"id":"irish-single-pot-still","name":"Irish single pot still","respell":"","family":"irish","terms":["single pot still","pot still","pure pot still"],"neighbour":"irish-single-malt","law":"The Irish whiskey technical file defines pot still Irish whiskey: a mash of at least 30 per cent malted barley and at least 30 per cent unmalted barley, with up to 5 per cent other unmalted cereals such as oats or rye, distilled in copper pot stills below 94.8% ABV and aged at least three years in wooden casks on the island of Ireland. Single means one distillery. A 2026 consultation on raising the other cereals to 30 per cent is a proposal, not the rule.","made":"Malted and unmalted barley are milled and mashed together, sometimes with a small share of oats or rye, then fermented and distilled in copper pot stills, three times at most houses, though the rule allows two. The raw barley gives an oily, creamy spirit with a spicy edge. It matures in wood, mostly ex-bourbon casks, with ex-sherry and other wine casks for richer styles.","taste":"A creamy, almost oily texture first, then spice: pepper, clove and ginger over green apple, pear, toasted cereal and honey. Sherry casks add dried fruit, nuts and toffee. The finish is long and warming, the spice lingering, and the whole is usually fuller and spicier than a blend.","lines":{"s10":"Irish single pot still is whiskey from malted and unmalted barley, pot distilled at one distillery: creamy, oily and spicy, with orchard fruit.","s20":"Single pot still is a style the Irish rules protect: malted and unmalted barley mashed together, distilled in copper pot stills at one distillery, usually three times. The raw barley gives a creamy, oily texture, with pepper, clove, green apple and honey.","s45":"This is an Irish single pot still whiskey, a style the Irish whiskey technical file protects: a mash of at least 30 per cent malted and 30 per cent unmalted barley, distilled in copper pot stills at one distillery, usually three times, and aged at least three years in wood in Ireland. The raw barley gives a creamy, oily texture, then pepper, clove and ginger over green apple, pear and honey; sherry casks add dried fruit. We pour it neat with a jug of water beside it, and it makes a fuller Irish Coffee. Before blends took over in the twentieth century, this was what Irish whiskey meant.","sale":"Single pot still is Ireland's own style: malted and raw barley together, so it is creamy and spicy at once. Taste it neat with a drop of water, or have it in an Irish Coffee that keeps its spice."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature, so the cream and the spice both come through.","water":"A few drops of still water lift the green apple and the pepper.","rocks":"One large cube if asked; cold thins the creamy texture.","highball":"With chilled ginger ale over ice and a lime wedge, a long drink that keeps the spice.","glass":"A Glencairn or tulip neat; a rocks glass for the cube; an Irish coffee glass for the coffee.","classic":"An Irish Coffee, where its spice stands up to the cream; a Tipperary or a Hot Toddy too."},"guest":[{"q":"What does pot still mean here?","a":"In Ireland it names a style as well as a still: a mash of malted and unmalted barley, distilled in copper pot stills. Single means it all comes from one distillery."},{"q":"Is it triple distilled?","a":"Usually, at most houses, but the technical file allows double or triple distillation. What the rule insists on is the mash of malted and unmalted barley and the copper pot stills."},{"q":"Why the unmalted barley?","a":"It gives the style its creamy, oily texture and its spice. A tax on malt from 1785 is often given as the reason distillers turned to raw barley, though historians debate how much it caused."}],"compare":"Against an Irish single malt: the malt is all malted barley, while single pot still adds at least 30 per cent unmalted barley, which brings a creamier, oilier texture and more spice. Both come from one distillery and copper pot stills; the malt tends to lighter fruit and cereal.","benchmarks":["Redbreast","Green Spot","Yellow Spot","Powers John's Lane","Teeling"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["irish"],"canon":["irish-coffee","tipperary","hot-toddy","cameron-s-kick"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What goes into the mash of an Irish single pot still whiskey, and what does single mean?","answer":"At least 30 per cent malted and at least 30 per cent unmalted barley, with up to 5 per cent other unmalted cereals, distilled in copper pot stills, usually three times. Single means one distillery. Creamy, oily and spicy, with orchard fruit."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"What minimum share of unmalted barley must a pot still Irish whiskey mash hold under the technical file?","options":["5 per cent","30 per cent","51 per cent","10 per cent"],"a":1,"explain":"30 per cent, beside at least 30 per cent malted barley; other unmalted cereals may make up to 5 per cent. A 2026 consultation proposes raising those other cereals to 30 per cent, a proposal only."},{"id":"q2","q":"Redbreast, Green Spot and Powers John's Lane are all distilled at which Irish distillery?","options":["Bushmills","Cooley","Kilbeggan","Midleton"],"a":3,"explain":"Midleton, in County Cork, where the stocks behind the style's revival were laid down."},{"id":"q3","q":"How many distillations does the technical file allow for a pot still Irish whiskey?","options":["Once only","Exactly three","Two or three","Four or more"],"a":2,"explain":"Two or three: the file allows double or triple distillation. Most houses triple distil, which is why many guests think it is the rule."}],"sources":[{"title":"European Commission, eAmbrosia: Irish Whiskey technical file","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/geographical-indications-register/eambrosia-public-api/api/v1/attachments/55944"},{"title":"Revenue: Whiskey verification checks","url":"https://www.revenue.ie/en/companies-and-charities/documents/excise/whiskey-verification-checks.pdf"},{"title":"Vinetur: Ireland opens review that could redefine Irish whiskey","url":"https://www.vinetur.com/en/20260629103448/ireland-opens-review-that-could-redefine-irish-whiskey.html"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Single pot still whiskey","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_pot_still_whiskey"},{"title":"The Whisky Exchange: Single pot still Irish whiskey, a complete guide","url":"https://www.thewhiskyexchange.com/inspiration/article/18566/single-pot-still-irish-whiskey-history-style-revival-the-whisky-exchange"},{"title":"VinePair: The history of pot still Irish whiskey","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/history-pot-still-irish-whiskey/"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Green Spot (whiskey)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Spot_(whiskey)"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Irish Whiskey, pot still, malt, grain and blend","url":""}]}
  ]
